    {
      "id": 1,
      "text": "Wagener was incorporated in 1888 and developed as a market town serving the agricultural communities of southeastern Aiken County.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.hereaiken.com/wagener/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The town of Wagener was named for George Wagener, who was a major supporter of the railroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aikencountysc.gov/206/Wagener-Salley-Perry"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Wagener was originally known as Pinder Town and later as Guntersville or Gunter's Crossroad.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aikencountysc.gov/206/Wagener-Salley-Perry"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "During the 1920s and 30s, asparagus grown in Wagener was exported across America.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        29
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.aikencountysc.gov/206/Wagener-Salley-Perry"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Cotton was once a successful product in Wagener, with huge bales lining the streets to await departure by train.",
